  "title": "Places --- and the Two Noun Families",
  "drivablePrefix": 6,
  "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:f8aab831273e5c65",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "SA-C16-house",
      "sequence": 730,
      "title": "गृहम् (gṛham) — house, home",
      "headword": "गृहम्",
      "romanization": "gṛham",
      "gloss": "house, home",
      "script": "devanagari",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:a7d6f918d909ac72",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You can describe a thing now — big, small, new, old. This chapter gives you places to put those things."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: गृहम्",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "गृहम् (gṛham) — \"house, home.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The traditional derivation ties it to the root √ग्रह् (grah), \"to seize, to hold.\" A house is the thing that holds you — the grasp you live inside. That is how Sanskrit's own grammarians heard the word, and it is worth hearing that way."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You can already stack an adjective in front of it: महत् गृहम् (gṛham), \"a big house\"; नव गृहम् (gṛham), \"a new house.\" The adjective comes first, as you have already seen it do."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "This is your first place. Everything in this chapter is somewhere you can be, and every one of them takes the adjectives you already own."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "gṛham — \"house\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *gṛham* — \"house\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"a big house\" — mahat gṛham",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"a big house\" — *mahat gṛham*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"a new house\" — nava gṛham",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"a new house\" — *nava gṛham*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What root is गृहम् (gṛham) traditionally tied to? (√ग्रह्, \"to seize, to hold.\") Where does the adjective go? (Before the noun.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "SA-C16-village",
      "sequence": 740,
      "title": "ग्रामः (grāmaḥ) — village",
      "headword": "ग्रामः",
      "romanization": "grāmaḥ",
      "gloss": "village",
      "script": "devanagari",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:887ab8ab8cc51a1a",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One house is a home. Many of them together are this."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ग्रामः",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ग्रामः (grāmaḥ) — \"village.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Its oldest sense is not \"village\" but \"a collection, a troop\" — a gathering of people considered as one body. The settlement took its name from the group, not the group from the settlement, which tells you how the word's first speakers saw the place: people first, buildings after."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Notice the ending. गृहम् (gṛham) ends in -am; ग्रामः (grāmaḥ) ends in -aḥ. Sanskrit sorts its nouns into families, and that final syllable is the badge. You do not need the system yet — only the observation that the endings differ, and that the difference is not random."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two places now, and your first glimpse of the noun families — the -am ending and the -aḥ ending, sitting side by side."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "grāmaḥ — \"village\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *grāmaḥ* — \"village\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"a big village\" — mahat grāmaḥ",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"a big village\" — *mahat grāmaḥ*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the two endings back to back — gṛham, grāmaḥ",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the two endings back to back — *gṛham*, *grāmaḥ*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What did ग्रामः (grāmaḥ) mean before it meant \"village\"? (A collection, a troop.) Which two endings have you now seen? (-am and -aḥ.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "SA-C16-road",
      "sequence": 750,
      "title": "मार्गः (mārgaḥ) — road, path",
      "headword": "मार्गः",
      "romanization": "mārgaḥ",
      "gloss": "road, path",
      "script": "devanagari",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:d5918b34cdb9641c",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two places, and no way between them. Here is the way."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: मार्गः",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "मार्गः (mārgaḥ) — \"road, path, way.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "From the root √मृग् (mṛg), \"to track, to hunt, to search out.\" A road is a tracking — the line left by someone who went looking. The same root gives मृगः (mṛgaḥ), \"deer\": the animal that gets tracked."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So the word does not name a built thing at all. It names the trace of a search, and only later the paved version of it. English keeps that metaphor alive when it speaks of a line of enquiry."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "मार्गः (mārgaḥ) takes the same -aḥ ending as ग्रामः (grāmaḥ). That is the second word in its family, and the pattern is becoming visible rather than asserted."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "mārgaḥ — \"road\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *mārgaḥ* — \"road\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"the old road\" — purātana mārgaḥ",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"the old road\" — *purātana mārgaḥ*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the related animal — mṛgaḥ, \"deer\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the related animal — *mṛgaḥ*, \"deer\"]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does √मृग् mean? (To track, to hunt.) What animal shares the root? (मृगः, the deer.) Which ending does मार्गः (mārgaḥ) take? (-aḥ.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "SA-C16-forest",
      "sequence": 760,
      "title": "वनम् (vanam) — forest, wood",
      "headword": "वनम्",
      "romanization": "vanam",
      "gloss": "forest, wood",
      "script": "devanagari",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:ffdcdef42c571946",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Follow the road far enough out of the village and you reach this."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: वनम्",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "वनम् (vanam) — \"forest, wood, wilderness.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "In the older literature it carries a weight that \"forest\" does not quite catch: वनम् (vanam) is where the settled world stops. To go to the वनम् (vanam) is to leave the ग्रामः (grāmaḥ) behind, and a great deal of Sanskrit narrative turns on somebody doing exactly that."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Its ending is -am, so it belongs with गृहम् (gṛham) — which gives the chapter a neat symmetry: the two -am words so far are the house you live inside and the wild you do not."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four places, and the two endings now have two words each. That is a pattern you have observed, not one you have been handed."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "vanam — \"forest\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *vanam* — \"forest\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"a big forest\" — mahat vanam",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"a big forest\" — *mahat vanam*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the two -am words together — gṛham, vanam",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the two *-am* words together — *gṛham*, *vanam*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does वनम् (vanam) mean beyond \"forest\"? (Where the settled world stops.) Which other word shares its ending? (गृहम् (gṛham).)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "SA-C16-city",
      "sequence": 770,
      "title": "नगरम् (nagaram) — city",
      "headword": "नगरम्",
      "romanization": "nagaram",
      "gloss": "city",
      "script": "devanagari",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:63e0700a96330862",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The last of the five places, and the biggest."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: नगरम्",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "नगरम् (nagaram) — \"city, town.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Its origin is genuinely unsettled, and that is worth saying plainly rather than inventing a story. Sanskrit offers no agreed root for it. Some scholars have proposed that it came into the language from outside — one of a handful of everyday words that may record a borrowing from languages spoken in the subcontinent before Sanskrit arrived. Others resist that reading. The honest position is that nobody knows."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Whatever its history, it ends in -am, like गृहम् (gṛham) and वनम् (vanam)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five places: house, village, road, forest, city. Three take -am and two take -aḥ, and the next lesson says what that division actually is."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "nagaram — \"city\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *nagaram* — \"city\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"a new city\" — nava nagaram",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"a new city\" — *nava nagaram*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "all three -am words — gṛham, vanam, nagaram",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: all three *-am* words — *gṛham*, *vanam*, *nagaram*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Why is नगरम् (nagaram) unusual among this chapter's words? (Its origin is unsettled; it may be a borrowing.) Which ending does it take? (-am.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "SA-C16-noun-families",
      "sequence": 780,
      "title": "-अम्, -अः (-am, -aḥ) — the two noun endings, side by side",
      "headword": "-अम् · -अः",
      "romanization": "-am · -aḥ",
      "gloss": "the two noun endings, side by side",
      "script": "devanagari",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:90c44b04a9b79625",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You have met five places and noticed their endings differ. This lesson names what you noticed."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: -अम्, -अः",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Sanskrit sorts its nouns into genders, and the ending you have been hearing is the clue:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "गृहम् (gṛham), वनम् (vanam), नगरम् (nagaram) end in -अम् — these are neuter. ग्रामः (grāmaḥ), मार्गः (mārgaḥ) end in -अः — these are masculine."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "This is the same machinery the adjectives used without naming it: they change to match. महत् गृहम् (gṛham) and महान् ग्रामः (grāmaḥ) are one adjective agreeing with two different families."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You do not need to produce that agreement yet. What you need is the ear for it — when you hear -अम् or -अः closing a word, you are hearing which family it belongs to, and Sanskrit will keep using that information for everything that follows."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You can now hear a noun's family from its ending, which is the single most useful thing to know about a Sanskrit noun."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the three neuters — gṛham, vanam, nagaram",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the three neuters — *gṛham*, *vanam*, *nagaram*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the two masculines — grāmaḥ, mārgaḥ",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the two masculines — *grāmaḥ*, *mārgaḥ*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "which family a new word ending in -am belongs to",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: which family a new word ending in *-am* belongs to]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which ending marks the neuter? (-अम्.) Which marks the masculine? (-अः.) What else in the sentence responds to a noun's family? (The adjective.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "findings": []
}
